#: src/rules/sections.js:68
msgid "Move: a normal chess move. A part of a ghost can move on its own; it then moves only in the possibilities where the piece is really on that square."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:69
msgid "Split: a knight, bishop, rook or queen moves to two squares at once. Both targets must be squares the piece could normally reach, and both must be empty for certain. Each target gets half of the chance the piece had. A split never captures and never rolls the dice."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:70
msgid "g1-f3|h3: the knight splits."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:71
msgid "Merge: choose two squares where the same ghost stands and a target both parts can reach. If the piece ends up on the target in every possibility, it is solid again. Merging onto an enemy piece is a converging capture: if the ghost has no other part, both paths are certainly clear and the enemy piece is surely there, the capture is certain. Preparation beats dice."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:72
msgid "d4|h5-h8: the queen merges onto the king. Captured for certain, no dice."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:73
msgid "Measure: point at one of your own ghosts. The game rolls to decide where it really is, using the odds on the board. The piece becomes solid there, and every linked piece updates. This uses your turn. You cannot measure enemy pieces."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:74
msgid "Measuring the knight: it turns out to be on f3 or on h3."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:80
msgid "3. What happens when you move"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:84
msgid "Preview"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:84 src/rules/sections.js:254
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:86
msgid "The result is fixed. No dice."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:87
msgid "No dice, but afterwards your piece is (or stays) in several places, or it becomes linked."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:88
msgid "The game rolls. The chance of every result is shown before you commit."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:89
msgid "Normally quantum, but your budget is full, so it is settled with a roll."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:92
msgid "The game rolls only when a move could turn out in more than one way and it lands on a square where a piece is or might be, or it is a pawn or king move, a Measure, or your budget is full. Nothing else ever rolls, and nothing collapses on its own."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:95
msgid "What happened"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:97
msgid "Your piece moved and took the piece on the target square."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:98
msgid "Your piece moved. The target square turned out to be empty."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:99
msgid "Nothing moved: your piece wasn’t really on its square, its way was blocked, or your own piece was on the target. Your turn is still used."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:102
msgid "A solid bishop lands on a ghost: 50% Captured, 50% Moved. Afterwards the board catches up."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:103
msgid "A ghost only hits if it is really there: a 50% part that attacks a solid piece captures half the time and misses otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:104
msgid "The queen’s d3 part attacks the rook: Captured or Missed."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:105
msgid "Passing a ghost links you. If you only pass a square where a piece might be, nothing is rolled: where the piece is in the way you stay, where it isn’t you arrive. Now the two pieces are linked, and finding out about one tells you about the other."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:106
msgid "a1-a8 passes a4: the rook is now 50% a1, 50% a8, linked to the knight."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:112
msgid "4. Pawns"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:117
msgid "Pawns are always solid. They never split or merge, and every pawn move is settled at once."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:118
msgid "Pushing onto a square where a piece might be is a roll: Moved means it was free, Missed means something was there."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:119
msgid "A double step needs both squares free. If the way is blocked, the pawn stays where it was."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:120
msgid "Capturing diagonally onto a ghost is a roll: Captured or Missed."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:121
msgid "Promotion happens only if the pawn really reaches the last rank. En passant works as in chess and is always certain."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:124
msgid "Pawns make great probes: e2-e4 finds out where the knight is."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:130
msgid "5. The king and castling"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:135
msgid "The king is always solid. A king step onto a square where a piece might be is a roll."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:136
msgid "There is no check. You may move your king into danger; if you do, your opponent may take it."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:137
msgid "King danger: every king shows the chance that the opponent could capture it with one move right now. At 100% the ring turns red."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:138
msgid "Safety net: if a move would leave your king at 10% danger or more and another move is at least 10 points safer, the game asks you to confirm."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:139
msgid "Your king cannot escape: if every move you could make would leave your king to be captured for certain, you have lost at once."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:140
msgid "Castling needs the king and rook solid on their starting squares and every square between them empty for certain. You may castle out of, through or into attack."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:143
msgid "Both parts of the queen attack the black king: king danger 100%."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:149
msgid "6. Winning and drawing"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:151
msgid "You win when you capture the enemy king, when the enemy king cannot escape after your move, or when your opponent resigns or, online, runs out of time."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:155
msgid "Draw: only the two kings are left."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:156
msgid "Draw: the same position appears for the third time."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:157
msgid "Draw: 50 moves by each side without a capture or a pawn move actually happening."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:158
msgid "Draw: the player to move has no legal move, or the game reaches 600 moves by each side."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:161
msgid "The first three draws wait if the player to move can capture the enemy king for certain. Players can also agree to a draw."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:167
msgid "7. Limits"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:169
msgid "The quantum budget counts the different ways your own pieces could be standing: one 50/50 knight gives 2, add a 50/50 bishop and you have 4, add a 50/50 rook and you have 8, which is full."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:170
msgid "Two white ghosts: White’s budget is 4/8."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:174
msgid "When your budget is full, splits are greyed out and quantum moves are settled with a roll. Everything else works as normal."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:175
msgid "Your opponent can never use up your budget; their moves can only lower it."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:176
msgid "A split may not spread a piece over more than 4 squares."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:184
msgid "8. Fair dice"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:189
msgid "Before you move, the preview shows the chance of every result."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:190
msgid "After a roll, the log shows a bar with one stretch per result (Missed, Moved, Captured) and a marker at the number rolled."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:191
msgid "Online games: the server draws a fresh random number when your move is applied. Every roll is recorded and chained, and changes to moves you have already seen show a warning."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:192
msgid "Local games remember each roll: undoing and playing the same move in the same position gives the same result, so undo cannot re-roll the dice. Undo marks the game as assisted."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:193
msgid "The built-in engine and AI opponents do not know future rolls. They see the same odds you do."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:201
msgid "9. Online games"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:206
msgid "You can resign at any time, and you can offer a draw."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:207
msgid "Online games have a time limit per move (1, 3 or 7 days). The player who runs out of time loses, unless the other player has only their king left (a draw) or the player had not moved yet (the game is cancelled)."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:208
msgid "In your running online games the coach, hints, the evaluation bar and the analysis are switched off. They come back when the game ends."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:209
msgid "Games against the computer, an AI model or pass & play never change your rating."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:217
msgid "10. What’s quantum about it (and what isn’t)"
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:219
msgid "Quantum Chess uses three real quantum ideas in a simplified form: superposition (a ghost is in several places until something asks), measurement (landing, pawn and king moves and Measure ask a question with the shown odds) and entanglement (linked pieces are correlated)."
msgstr ""

#: src/rules/sections.js:220
msgid "It does not model interference or non-locality, and it shows you the full state the way a physics simulator would. Turn on physics names in the settings to see the textbook terms."
msgstr ""

